#1.4 Git Installation & Fundamentals

## 1. Install and verify Git

Git is installed on my Windows computer. I verified the installation with:

```text
git version 2.53.0.windows.3
```

## 2. Configure Git username and email

Run these commands with your own name and email:

```bash
git config --global user.name "Your Name"
git config --global user.email "your-email@example.com"
```

Verify the settings:

```bash
git config --global --get user.name
git config --global --get user.email
```

## 3. Create the Recipe Collection repository

I created a `Recipe Collection` folder, initialized it as a Git repository, and added a sample `recipes.txt` file.

```bash
cd "Recipe Collection"
git init
```

The `.git` directory contains Git metadata such as `HEAD`, `config`, `description`, `index`, and the `hooks`, `info`, `logs`, `objects`, and `refs` folders.

## 4. Clone a public music repository

I tried to clone a public Spotify analysis repository:

```bash
git clone https://github.com/alexfunking/spotify-music-analysis.git
```

The clone failed because GitHub HTTPS authentication was unavailable in my environment, so I could not list the cloned files. Once access is available, list the files with:

```bash
cd spotify-music-analysis
dir
```

## 5. Make five atomic wishlist commits

I added one item to `wishlist.txt` per commit:

```text
Try a new cafe
Watch a new movie
Cook a new recipe
Read a novel
Visit a museum
```

The commits are:

```text
51ef3e9 Add cafe visit to wishlist
fd4ca08 Add movie to wishlist
6ea5cbe Add cooking idea to wishlist
2e46468 Add book to wishlist
1adb1b9 Add museum visit to wishlist
```

To push the commits, create a new GitHub repository and run:

```bash
git remote add origin https://github.com/YOUR-USERNAME/Recipe-Collection.git
git branch -M main
git push -u origin main
```

Replace `YOUR-USERNAME` with your GitHub username. The commits are local; pushing requires GitHub authentication.